  note      = {PMID 35496. Reference equation for the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve; canonical P50 at PaO2 $\approx$ 27 mmHg corresponds to SpO2 = 50 percent. Corroborated 2026-07-27 in West's Respiratory Physiology 11th ed., p. 200: "The normal value for human blood is about 27 mm Hg", anchor point "P50: 27, SO2 50\%". Read at source 2026-07-28: the PubMed abstract carries the equations, verbatim Eq. (1) "S = (((Po2(3) + 150 Po2)(-1) x 23,400) + 1)(-1)", which evaluates to P50 = 26.86 mmHg, S(27) = 0.5035, S(20) = 0.3198. The 26.6 mmHg once recorded in the companion paper.bib was not invented -- it is the Bohr-coefficient normalisation in the paper's Eq. (4) -- but it is not the P50 of Eq. (1) and must not be reinstated as one. OPEN [2026-07-28]: the SpO2 $\approx$ 0.30 stated at PaO2 = 20 mmHg (manuscript.md L1095, supplement.tex L221) is 0.3198 by this equation; flagged for author decision, nothing changed.}
